Below is the graph of $y = a \sin bx$ for some constants $a < 0$ and $b > 0.$  Redraw the graph for $a = -3$ and $b = \frac{1}{2}$, and find $a.$

[asy]
import TrigMacros;

size(400);

real g(real x)
{
    return (-3*sin(0.5*x));
}

draw(graph(g,-12*pi,12*pi,n=700,join=operator ..),red);
trig_axes(-12*pi,12*pi,-4,4,3*pi,1);
layer();
rm_trig_labels(-5, 5, 2);

label("$3$", (0,3), E);
label("$-3$", (0,-3), E);
[/asy]

In Zhangjiajie, a scenic area is being renovated and upgraded to improve economic efficiency, thereby expanding domestic demand and increasing tourism value. After market research, the added value of tourism \(y\) (in ten thousand yuan) and the investment \(x(x\geqslant 10)\) (in ten thousand yuan) satisfy the relationship: \(y=f(x)=ax^{2}+ \dfrac {101}{50}x-b\ln \dfrac {x}{10}\), where \(a\) and \(b\) are constants. When \(x=10\) ten thousand yuan, \(y=19.2\) ten thousand yuan; when \(x=20\) ten thousand yuan, \(y=35.7\) ten thousand yuan. (Reference data: \(\ln 2=0.7\), \(\ln 3=1.1\), \(\ln 5=1.6\))  
